## 2. Configuring & Executing Vulnerability Scans

### Scan Sensitivity & Safety
* Build custom scans by tailoring base vendor templates to balance deep discovery with system stability.
* Disable unnecessary plugins to optimize scan runtimes and reduce network overhead.
* Carefully evaluate and isolate **intrusive/unsafe plugins** before running them against production workloads.

### Network vs. Credentialed vs. Agent-Based Scanning

| Scanning Method | Architecture | Strengths | Trade-offs |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Network (Uncredentialed)** | Probes target hosts remotely across network boundaries | Simulates external attacker view; zero host configuration needed | Obstructed by firewalls/IPS; high false-positive rate; blind to local patch states |
| **Credentialed** | Scanner logs into the target OS/database with valid credentials | High-fidelity visibility into configurations, installed packages, and local patches | Requires privileged account management across target fleets |
| **Agent-Based** | Lightweight client software installed directly on the host | Continuous "inside-out" monitoring; independent of network controls or host connectivity | Overhead of agent lifecycle management across diverse operating systems |

### Scan Perspectives
* **External:** The perspective of an unauthenticated attacker probing from the public Internet.
* **Internal:** The perspective of an insider or compromised workstation inside the corporate network.
* **Datacenter / DMZ / Local Host:** Probes placed behind local firewalls, proxies, and IDS/IPS controls to assess raw host configuration.

### Scanner Maintenance
* **Scanner Engine Updates:** Keep scanner software patched to prevent exploitation of the security tool itself.
* **Vulnerability Plugin Feeds:** Update signature and test plugin feeds daily to detect newly published Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVEs).

